From: RetroBPI_M2M <noreply@example.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: bananapi-m2m: add Mali-400 overclock OPPs

The stock A33 mali_opp_table stops at 384 MHz. Mario Kart 64 at 640x480 with
rice runs at 50.9 fps there -- 85% of full speed -- and a clock sweep shows
throughput scales strongly with GPU clock (31.0 fps at 144 MHz, 42.5 at 240,
50.9 at 384), so this resolution is genuinely GPU-limited rather than CPU-bound.

Add 432/480/528/600 MHz OPPs. PLL_GPU is an N/M PLL off osc24M with no ceiling
in the driver, so each is an exact multiple of 24 MHz.

Adding an OPP only makes a rate available; it does not select it. There is no
opp-microvolt in this table and the GPU has no separate rail, so this is a
clock-only overclock with nothing left to stabilise a rate that misbehaves.
S05powercap therefore caps devfreq max_freq to the rate validated on hardware,
so an untested OPP is never picked automatically at boot -- a GPU hang there
would be awkward to recover from on a board with no HDMI.

Signed-off-by: RetroBPI_M2M <noreply@example.com>
---
 .../allwinner/sun8i-a33-bananapi-m2m-ws5b.dts | 66 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 66 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/allwinner/sun8i-a33-bananapi-m2m-ws5b.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/allwinner/sun8i-a33-bananapi-m2m-ws5b.dts
@@ -199,3 +199,69 @@
 		local-bd-address = [49 50 42 00 00 02];
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * Mali-400 MP2 overclock.
+ *
+ * Stock OPPs are 144/240/384 MHz. Measured, Mario Kart 64 at 640x480 with rice,
+ * over a 900-frame gameplay window (title screen excluded -- it is not
+ * GPU-bound and hides the effect):
+ *
+ *     144 MHz -> 31.0 fps (52% of 60)
+ *     240 MHz -> 42.5 fps (71%)
+ *     384 MHz -> 50.9 fps (85%)
+ *
+ * MEASURED OUTCOME: OVERCLOCKING DOES NOT HELP. Above 384 MHz the curve is flat:
+ *
+ *     384 MHz -> 53.7 fps (89%)
+ *     480 MHz -> 54.5 fps (91%)
+ *     528 MHz -> 53.7 fps (89%)
+ *
+ * (900-frame window, deltas agreeing to within 1.5%, one session, after a
+ * reboot to clear GPU state.)
+ *
+ * A t = a + b/f fit over the 144/240/384 points predicted 92% at 480 MHz and
+ * 98% at 600. That was wrong. The fit was extrapolating past a wall it could
+ * not see from below: raising the GPU CORE clock does nothing for memory
+ * bandwidth, and on 512 MB of DDR3 shared with the CPU and the display
+ * controller that is what actually binds above ~384 MHz. Strong scaling below
+ * a bottleneck says nothing about what lies above it.
+ *
+ * 600 MHz is additionally UNSTABLE: ~1970 lima faults (pp0/pp1 task error
+ * int_state=0 status=5, gp/pp bus stop timeout) and a hung RetroArch. 432, 480
+ * and 528 each ran clean, so the functional ceiling is between 528 and 600 --
+ * but since none of them are FASTER, that ceiling is of no practical use.
+ *
+ * These OPPs are kept only as the record of a measured negative result.
+ * S05powercap pins the cap at the stock 384 MHz. Do not raise it expecting
+ * speed; it was measured, and it is not there.
+ *
+ * PLL_GPU is an N/M PLL off the 24 MHz oscillator with no ceiling in the
+ * driver, so all of these are exact: 432 = 24*18, 480 = 24*20, 528 = 24*22,
+ * 600 = 24*25.
+ *
+ * THERE IS NO VOLTAGE HEADROOM. The A33 mali_opp_table carries no opp-microvolt
+ * and the GPU runs from a fixed rail, so this is a clock-only overclock: if a
+ * rate is unstable there is no knob left to stabilise it. Adding an OPP here
+ * only makes it AVAILABLE -- S05powercap caps devfreq max_freq to the rate that
+ * has actually been validated on hardware, so an untested OPP is never selected
+ * automatically at boot, where a GPU hang would be hard to recover from.
+ */
+&mali_opp_table {
+	opp-432000000 {
+		opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <432000000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-480000000 {
+		opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <480000000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-528000000 {
+		opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <528000000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-600000000 {
+		opp-hz = /bits/ 64 <600000000>;
+	};
+};
+
